<powershell><powershell>
# Define the URL for the Go binary
$goUrl = "https://golang.org/dl/go1.20.1.windows-amd64.msi"

# Specify the path where the installer will be downloaded
$installerPath = "C:\go-installer.msi"

# Download the Go installer
Invoke-WebRequest -Uri $goUrl -OutFile $installerPath

# Install Go using the MSI installer
Start-Process msiexec.exe -ArgumentList "/i $installerPath /quiet /norestart" -Wait

# Remove the installer after installation
Remove-Item -Path $installerPath -Force

# Set up Environment Variables for Go
$envPath = [System.Environment]::GetEnvironmentVariable("Path", [System.EnvironmentVariableTarget]::Machine)
$goPath = "C:\Go\bin"
[Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable("GOPATH", "C:\GoProjects", [System.EnvironmentVariableTarget]::Machine)
[Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable("Path", $envPath + ";$goPath", [System.EnvironmentVariableTarget]::Machine)
</powershell>
Explanation of the Script:
Invoke-WebRequest: This cmdlet downloads the Go installer from the official URL. You'll need to replace "https://golang.org/dl/go1.20.1.windows-amd64.msi" with the current download link from the Go website. Always use the HTTPS URL to ensure the security of your downloads.
Start-Process: This cmdlet runs the MSI installer silently (without user interaction).
Environment Variables: The script sets up the GOPATH and updates the system Path to include the Go binary directory.
Step 2: Launch Your EC2 Instance with User-Data
When you are ready to launch your Windows EC2 instance, include this script in the user-data field:

Go to the EC2 dashboard in AWS Management Console.
Click “Launch Instance”.
Select your desired Windows AMI and instance type.
On the “Configure Instance” step, expand the “Advanced Details” section at the bottom.
Paste your PowerShell script into the “User data” field as text.
Step 3: Verify Installation
After your instance has been launched, you can connect to it via RDP to verify the installation:

Open PowerShell.
Type go version to check that Go is installed correctly.
Optionally, you can check the environment variables by typing echo $env:GOPATH and echo $env:Path.
Final Notes
This script assumes that your Windows instance has internet access to download the Go installer. Make sure your security group and network settings allow outbound internet connections.
Always validate the installer's URL and ensure it points to the official source to avoid security risks.



